1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,680
This burger costs about three dollars to make.

2
00:00:02,930 --> 00:00:04,146
It sells for ten.

3
00:00:04,396 --> 00:00:07,532
This soda? About thirty cents. It sells for three dollars.

4
00:00:07,782 --> 00:00:09,915
So a restaurant is easy money, right?

5
00:00:10,165 --> 00:00:11,253
Not so fast.

6
00:00:11,753 --> 00:00:14,292
Meet Tony. He just opened Tony's Burgers.

7
00:00:14,542 --> 00:00:19,513
But out of every ten dollars, rent takes a bite. Then staff. Then power, gas and repairs.

8
00:00:19,763 --> 00:00:22,131
Tony often keeps less than one dollar.

9
00:00:22,631 --> 00:00:24,423
So why not buy a famous name?

10
00:00:24,673 --> 00:00:28,385
Opening a big burger franchise can cost over a million dollars.

11
00:00:28,635 --> 00:00:30,789
Plus a cut of every single sale.

12
00:00:31,289 --> 00:00:34,084
So Tony keeps his own name. But nobody knows it.

13
00:00:34,334 --> 00:00:37,214
Down the street, the famous chain has a line out the door.

14
00:00:37,464 --> 00:00:39,320
Tony's dining room? Empty.

15
00:00:39,570 --> 00:00:42,877
He hands out flyers. Posts videos. Gives free samples.

16
00:00:43,127 --> 00:00:47,287
About one in four new restaurants close or change hands in their first year.

17
00:00:47,787 --> 00:00:51,712
Making food is cheap. Getting people to pick your food? That's the hard part.

18
00:00:51,962 --> 00:00:55,034
Tony wins them over one happy customer at a time.

